      "content": "The argument that strong union power can create economic friction is a well-discussed critique, often focusing on the impact of higher labor costs and rigid work rules on market competition and small business flexibility.\n\nHowever, proponents argue that the \"unleashing of power\" that the unions provided was necessary to correct inherent market power imbalances, arguing that the initial gains in stability and fair treatment for the majority were a necessary and positive societal trade-off to intense, unregulated capital expansion.",
